1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,083
You've been walking through the forest since morning.

2
00:00:03,083 --> 00:00:04,624
Your stepmother sent you.

3
00:00:04,624 --> 00:00:06,936
The fire at home went out,

4
00:00:06,936 --> 00:00:10,789
and she told you to go and borrow a light.

5
00:00:10,789 --> 00:00:11,560
From her.

6
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:15,094
And now you've found the place.

7
00:00:15,094 --> 00:00:17,184
A little wooden hut.

8
00:00:17,184 --> 00:00:20,318
Standing on two enormous chicken legs.

9
00:00:20,318 --> 00:00:22,930
Turning, slowly, round and round.

10
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:25,587
Around it, a fence.

11
00:00:25,587 --> 00:00:27,280
Not wood. Bones.

12
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:30,666
Human bones. And on every post,

13
00:00:30,666 --> 00:00:35,180
a skull, with something burning inside its eyes.

14
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:39,977
You're standing at the gate when the trees start to groan,

15
00:00:39,977 --> 00:00:42,376
and the wind starts to howl,

16
00:00:42,376 --> 00:00:45,174
and something comes flying over the forest.

17
00:00:45,174 --> 00:00:46,373
An old woman.

18
00:00:46,373 --> 00:00:47,972
Riding in a mortar.

19
00:00:47,972 --> 00:00:50,770
Sweeping away her tracks with a broom.

20
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:53,112
And she sniffs the air.

21
00:00:53,112 --> 00:00:56,220
And she says, I smell a human soul.

22
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:58,312
She is Baba Yaga.

23
00:00:58,312 --> 00:00:59,582
She eats people.

24
00:00:59,582 --> 00:01:01,697
She also, sometimes, helps them.

25
00:01:01,697 --> 00:01:06,351
And the only thing that decides which one you get is

26
00:01:06,351 --> 00:01:08,890
how well you know her rules.

27
00:01:09,290 --> 00:01:11,815
This is the Ward Book.

28
00:01:11,815 --> 00:01:15,350
And tonight, how to survive Baba Yaga.

29
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:19,157
Baba Yaga comes from Slavic folk tales,

30
00:01:19,157 --> 00:01:24,511
and most of what we know about her comes from one

31
00:01:24,511 --> 00:01:29,866
man. Alexander Afanasyev, who between eighteen fifty-five and eighteen sixty-three published

32
00:01:29,866 --> 00:01:35,220
hundreds of Russian folk tales, collected from storytellers across the empire.

33
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:38,583
In those tales she flies in a mortar,

34
00:01:38,583 --> 00:01:41,176
the heavy bowl used to grind grain,

35
00:01:41,176 --> 00:01:43,399
and steers it with the pestle.

36
00:01:43,399 --> 00:01:45,991
She has iron teeth in some tellings,

37
00:01:45,991 --> 00:01:48,214
and one leg made of bone.

38
00:01:48,214 --> 00:01:51,177
Her nose is so long, one story says,

39
00:01:51,177 --> 00:01:53,770
it touches the ceiling of her hut.

40
00:01:54,170 --> 00:01:56,403
And the hut is alive.

41
00:01:56,403 --> 00:02:01,317
It stands on chicken legs, and it turns to face whoever

42
00:02:01,317 --> 00:02:04,890
it pleases. Usually, that means away from you.

43
00:02:05,290 --> 00:02:10,255
But here's what makes her different from a normal fairy tale

44
00:02:10,255 --> 00:02:13,414
witch. Baba Yaga isn't always the villain.

45
00:02:13,414 --> 00:02:16,122
In some tales she eats children.

46
00:02:16,122 --> 00:02:21,087
In others, she gives the hero exactly what he needs to

47
00:02:21,087 --> 00:02:23,343
win. Folklorists call her ambiguous.

48
00:02:23,343 --> 00:02:25,600
You can call her unpredictable.

49
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:30,560
Ward one. Know the words to turn the hut.

50
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:35,020
The hut on chicken legs usually stands with its back to

51
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:37,603
you, its door facing the deep forest.

52
00:02:37,603 --> 00:02:41,294
You can walk around it as much as you like.

53
00:02:41,294 --> 00:02:42,770
It just keeps turning.

54
00:02:43,170 --> 00:02:46,909
But the heroes of the tales know the words.

55
00:02:46,909 --> 00:02:48,570
Little hut, little hut.

56
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:51,063
Turn your back to the forest,

57
00:02:51,063 --> 00:02:53,140
and your front to me.

58
00:02:53,540 --> 00:02:55,305
And the hut obeys.

59
00:02:55,305 --> 00:03:00,160
It turns, creaking, and the door swings round to face you.

60
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:04,132
Without the words, you're stuck outside in the dark.

61
00:03:04,132 --> 00:03:05,897
With them, you're in.

62
00:03:05,897 --> 00:03:10,310
Whether getting in is a good idea is another question.

63
00:03:10,710 --> 00:03:14,980
Ward two. Make her be a host.

64
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:18,602
In the tale of the Frog Princess,

65
00:03:18,602 --> 00:03:21,365
Prince Ivan walks into the hut,

66
00:03:21,365 --> 00:03:24,127
and Baba Yaga starts asking questions.

67
00:03:24,127 --> 00:03:26,429
And Ivan does something amazing.

68
00:03:26,429 --> 00:03:28,270
He tells her off.

69
00:03:28,670 --> 00:03:33,217
He says, more or less: first give a traveller food and

70
00:03:33,217 --> 00:03:37,350
drink, and a hot bath, and then ask your questions.

71
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:39,501
And she does it.

72
00:03:39,501 --> 00:03:43,880
She feeds him, bathes him, and then she helps him.

73
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:48,258
In the old Russian world, hospitality was a sacred duty.

74
00:03:48,258 --> 00:03:51,323
Once you've eaten someone's bread and salt,

75
00:03:51,323 --> 00:03:52,636
they're your host.

76
00:03:52,636 --> 00:03:55,701
And a host doesn't eat their guest.

77
00:03:55,701 --> 00:03:57,890
At least, not right away.

78
00:03:58,290 --> 00:04:03,350
Ward three. Be kind to everything in her house.

79
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:08,384
There's another tale, retold in English by Arthur Ransome in nineteen

80
00:04:08,384 --> 00:04:12,176
sixteen. A girl is sent to Baba Yaga's hut,

81
00:04:12,176 --> 00:04:16,810
but before she goes, her kind aunt gives her some advice.

82
00:04:17,210 --> 00:04:20,295
When the gate squeaks, she oils its hinges.

83
00:04:20,295 --> 00:04:23,381
When the dogs snarl, she gives them bread.

84
00:04:23,381 --> 00:04:26,081
When the birch tree whips her face,

85
00:04:26,081 --> 00:04:28,395
she ties a ribbon on it.

86
00:04:28,395 --> 00:04:31,480
And when the starving cat looks at her,

87
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:34,180
she gives it a piece of meat.

88
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:37,451
Later that night, the girl runs.

89
00:04:37,451 --> 00:04:42,236
And Baba Yaga screams at her household to stop her.

90
00:04:42,236 --> 00:04:43,672
Gate, slam shut!

91
00:04:43,672 --> 00:04:45,586
Dogs, tear her apart!

92
00:04:45,586 --> 00:04:48,457
Birch tree, whip her eyes out!

93
00:04:48,457 --> 00:04:50,850
And none of them move.

94
00:04:51,250 --> 00:04:54,686
The gate says, you've never oiled my hinges,

95
00:04:54,686 --> 00:04:55,974
but she did.

96
00:04:55,974 --> 00:05:00,269
The dogs say, you never gave us anything but crusts,

97
00:05:00,269 --> 00:05:02,417
but she gave us bread.

98
00:05:02,417 --> 00:05:04,994
They've served Baba Yaga for years.

99
00:05:04,994 --> 00:05:08,430
One act of kindness, and they changed sides.

100
00:05:08,830 --> 00:05:10,395
Even the cat helps.

101
00:05:10,395 --> 00:05:13,917
It gives the girl a towel and a comb.

102
00:05:13,917 --> 00:05:17,829
When Baba Yaga chases her, she throws down the towel,

103
00:05:17,829 --> 00:05:20,177
and it becomes a wide river.

104
00:05:20,177 --> 00:05:24,481
She throws down the comb, and it becomes a forest so

105
00:05:24,481 --> 00:05:27,220
thick even a witch can't get through.

106
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:32,270
Ward four. Keep your mother's blessing close.

107
00:05:32,670 --> 00:05:36,239
In the most famous Baba Yaga story of all,

108
00:05:36,239 --> 00:05:40,600
Vasilisa the Beautiful, the heroine's mother dies when she's a little

109
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:44,962
girl. But before she dies, she gives Vasilisa a tiny wooden

110
00:05:44,962 --> 00:05:46,548
doll, and a secret.

111
00:05:46,548 --> 00:05:49,720
Feed the doll, and it will help you.

112
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:52,918
Baba Yaga gives Vasilisa impossible jobs.

113
00:05:52,918 --> 00:05:54,318
Clean the yard.

114
00:05:54,318 --> 00:05:55,717
Cook the dinner.

115
00:05:55,717 --> 00:05:57,116
Wash the linen.

116
00:05:57,116 --> 00:06:02,247
Pick the mouldy grains out of a whole bushel of wheat.

117
00:06:02,247 --> 00:06:07,377
And every time, the little doll gets the job done while

118
00:06:07,377 --> 00:06:08,310
Vasilisa sleeps.

119
00:06:08,710 --> 00:06:13,694
Finally, Baba Yaga demands to know how she did it.

120
00:06:13,694 --> 00:06:15,688
And Vasilisa answers honestly.

121
00:06:15,688 --> 00:06:18,180
My mother's blessing helps me.

122
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:21,790
And Baba Yaga throws her out.

123
00:06:21,790 --> 00:06:23,930
Get out, she shouts.

124
00:06:23,930 --> 00:06:27,140
I want no blessed ones here.

125
00:06:27,540 --> 00:06:30,438
But she doesn't send her away empty-handed.

126
00:06:30,438 --> 00:06:33,751
She gives Vasilisa the fire she came for.

127
00:06:33,751 --> 00:06:37,063
A skull on a stick, with burning eyes.

128
00:06:37,063 --> 00:06:41,618
And when Vasilisa gets home, those eyes stare at the cruel

129
00:06:41,618 --> 00:06:44,930
stepmother and stepsisters, and burn them to ash.

130
00:06:45,330 --> 00:06:49,425
And the final ward, the most important.

131
00:06:49,425 --> 00:06:52,350
Don't ask too many questions.

132
00:06:52,750 --> 00:06:55,310
While Vasilisa stays at the hut,

133
00:06:55,310 --> 00:06:57,017
she sees three riders.

134
00:06:57,017 --> 00:07:00,431
One all in white, one all in red,

135
00:07:00,431 --> 00:07:02,138
one all in black.

136
00:07:02,138 --> 00:07:03,419
Baba Yaga explains.

137
00:07:03,419 --> 00:07:05,979
The white rider is the day.

138
00:07:05,979 --> 00:07:08,540
The red rider is the sun.

139
00:07:08,540 --> 00:07:11,100
The black rider is the night.

140
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:14,216
But Vasilisa also saw something else.

141
00:07:14,216 --> 00:07:16,931
Pairs of hands, with no bodies,

142
00:07:16,931 --> 00:07:20,099
floating in the hut, grinding the grain.

143
00:07:20,099 --> 00:07:23,720
And she does not ask about the hands.

144
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:26,524
And Baba Yaga is pleased.

145
00:07:26,524 --> 00:07:30,852
Because, she says, not every question leads to good.

146
00:07:30,852 --> 00:07:35,180
If you know too much, you'll soon grow old.

147
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:36,877
Curiosity is good.

148
00:07:36,877 --> 00:07:40,334
Prying into a witch's private business is not.

149
00:07:40,334 --> 00:07:42,928
Three questions, about the outside world,

150
00:07:42,928 --> 00:07:46,818
fine. Ask about what goes on inside her house,

151
00:07:46,818 --> 00:07:51,140
and it may be the last thing you ever learn.

152
00:07:51,540 --> 00:07:55,112
So where does Baba Yaga really come from?

153
00:07:55,112 --> 00:07:57,791
Her name itself is a puzzle.

154
00:07:57,791 --> 00:08:00,470
Baba means old woman, or grandmother.

155
00:08:00,470 --> 00:08:02,702
Yaga, nobody is quite sure.

156
00:08:02,702 --> 00:08:06,721
Scholars have linked it to old words for pain,

157
00:08:06,721 --> 00:08:08,060
anger and horror.

158
00:08:08,460 --> 00:08:12,104
And that chicken-legged hut might have a real-world cousin.

159
00:08:12,104 --> 00:08:16,558
Some writers compare it to the little wooden storehouses that forest

160
00:08:16,558 --> 00:08:18,987
peoples built high on tree stumps,

161
00:08:18,987 --> 00:08:21,416
to keep food safe from animals.

162
00:08:21,416 --> 00:08:25,870
From a distance, they really do look like a house standing

163
00:08:25,870 --> 00:08:26,680
on legs.

164
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:30,825
The great Russian folklorist Vladimir Propp had a darker idea.

165
00:08:30,825 --> 00:08:34,945
He saw Baba Yaga's hut as a border post between the

166
00:08:34,945 --> 00:08:38,691
world of the living and the world of the dead.

167
00:08:38,691 --> 00:08:41,313
The hero has to pass through it,

168
00:08:41,313 --> 00:08:43,560
be tested, and come out changed.

169
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:46,628
In Propp's famous study of fairy tales,

170
00:08:46,628 --> 00:08:49,676
she plays a role he called the donor.

171
00:08:49,676 --> 00:08:51,963
The figure who tests the hero,

172
00:08:51,963 --> 00:08:55,774
and if they pass, gives them the magic they need.

173
00:08:55,774 --> 00:08:58,060
Fail the test, and you're dinner.

174
00:08:58,460 --> 00:09:02,753
Her first clear appearance in print is in seventeen fifty-five,

175
00:09:02,753 --> 00:09:04,900
in Mikhail Lomonosov's Russian Grammar.

176
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:08,764
Then, at the very end of the eighteen hundreds,

177
00:09:08,764 --> 00:09:13,057
the artist Ivan Bilibin drew her for Vasilisa the Beautiful,

178
00:09:13,057 --> 00:09:17,350
and gave her the look that still defines her today.

179
00:09:17,750 --> 00:09:20,989
So, how do you survive Baba Yaga?

180
00:09:20,989 --> 00:09:23,303
Look at the wards again.

181
00:09:23,303 --> 00:09:24,691
Know the words.

182
00:09:24,691 --> 00:09:27,005
Make her be a host.

183
00:09:27,005 --> 00:09:29,318
Be kind to the cat.

184
00:09:29,318 --> 00:09:31,632
Keep your mother's blessing close.

185
00:09:31,632 --> 00:09:33,020
And don't pry.

186
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:37,823
Every single one of those is really a rule about how

187
00:09:37,823 --> 00:09:41,025
to behave. Be polite, but not a pushover.

188
00:09:41,025 --> 00:09:43,027
Be kind to the weak.

189
00:09:43,027 --> 00:09:45,429
Remember the people who loved you.

190
00:09:45,429 --> 00:09:47,430
Know when to stop asking.

191
00:09:47,830 --> 00:09:50,352
Baba Yaga doesn't punish the brave.

192
00:09:50,352 --> 00:09:52,874
She punishes the rude, the greedy,

193
00:09:52,874 --> 00:09:54,135
and the nosy.

194
00:09:54,135 --> 00:09:58,759
Which, to be fair, is more than you can say for

195
00:09:58,759 --> 00:09:59,600
most monsters.

196
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,630
Next week is the last ward of the year,

197
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:04,840
and the biggest.

198
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:07,260
On the darkest nights of winter,

199
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:10,083
something rides across the sky over Europe.

200
00:10:10,083 --> 00:10:14,520
Hounds, horses, ghosts, and a leader who might be a god.

201
00:10:14,920 --> 00:10:17,160
It's the Wild Hunt.

202
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:20,520
And if you hear it overhead,

203
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:23,880
there's one thing you must never,

204
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:30,040
ever do. Subscribe, so the Ward Book is open when it

205
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:34,520
rides. And until then, be kind to cats.
